,"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/inspect#collection-options","content":"Runtime behavior is governed by the options used when opening the collection:","locale":"en"},"896":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/inspect-33","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/inspect","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/inspect#collection-options","content":"`enable_mmap: 1/true` → Memory-mapped I/O is enabled for faster access.","locale":"en"},"897":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/inspect-34","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/inspect","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/inspect#collection-options","content":"`read_only: 0/false` → The collection is open for both reads and writes.","locale":"en"},"898":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/inspect-35","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/inspect","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/inspect#collection-path","content":"The `path` property returns the on-disk location of the collection:","locale":"en"},"899":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/inspect-36","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/inspect","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/inspect#collection-path","content":"This is the same path passed to `open()`.","locale":"en"},"900":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open","type":"page","content":"Open","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Products","Zvec","Guides","Collections"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/open","locale":"en"},"901":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open-0","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/open#usage","content":"Usage","locale":"en"},"902":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open-1","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/open#parameters","content":"Parameters","locale":"en"},"903":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open-2","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/open","content":"To open an existing collection, use the `open()` function to load it from disk.","locale":"en"},"904":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open-3","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/open","content":"The specified path **must point to an existing Zvec collection**. If no valid collection is found, `open()` will raise an error.","locale":"en"},"905":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open-4","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/open#parameters","content":"`path`: The filesystem path to the collection directory.","locale":"en"},"906":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open-5","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/open#parameters","content":"`option`: Settings that control runtime behavior.","locale":"en"},"907":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open-6","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/open#parameters","content":"`read_only`: Opens the collection in read-only mode. Attempts to write will raise an error.","locale":"en"},"908":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open-7","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/open#parameters","content":"Use read-only mode when sharing a collection across multiple processes — it ensures safe concurrent access without risking data corruption.","locale":"en"},"909":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open-8","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/open#parameters","content":"`enable_mmap`: Uses memory-mapped I/O for faster access (defaults to `True`). This trades slightly higher memory cache usage for improved performance.","locale":"en"},"910":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","type":"page","content":"Optimize","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Products","Zvec","Guides","Collections"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","locale":"en"},"911":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-0","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#why-optimization-is-needed","content":"Why Optimization is Needed","locale":"en"},"912":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-1","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#usage-example","content":"Usage Example","locale":"en"},"913":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-2","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#check-indexing-status","content":"Check Indexing Status","locale":"en"},"914":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-3","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#when-to-call-optimize","content":"When to Call `optimize()`","locale":"en"},"915":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-4","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","content":"The `optimize()` method **improves search performance** by building the configured vector index from vectors accumulated in a temporary flat buffer. It runs **in the background** and **does not block reads or writes**, ensuring your application remains fully responsive.","locale":"en"},"916":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-5","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#why-optimization-is-needed","content":"In Zvec, newly inserted vectors are **not added directly** to the configured vector index. Instead, they are first staged in a lightweight flat index (brute-force) buffer.","locale":"en"},"917":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-6","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#why-optimization-is-needed","content":"This design choice offers important benefits — but also a trade-off:","locale":"en"},"918":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-7","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#why-optimization-is-needed","content":"✅ **Strengths**","locale":"en"},"919":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-8","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#why-optimization-is-needed","content":"**Maximum write throughput**: Enables high-speed data ingestion.","locale":"en"},"920":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-9","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#why-optimization-is-needed","content":"**Streaming inserts**: Supports real-time insertion for index types like IVF that don't natively allow incremental updates.","locale":"en"},"921":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-10","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#why-optimization-is-needed","content":"⚠️ **Trade-off**","locale":"en"},"922":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-11","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#why-optimization-is-needed","content":"**Slower searches over time**: As the flat buffer grows, search performance degrades.","locale":"en"},"923":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-12","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#why-optimization-is-needed","content":"🔁 **Solution**","locale":"en"},"924":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-13","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#why-optimization-is-needed","content":"Call `optimize()` periodically. This triggers a background worker that merges the staged vectors into the configured vector index — **without interrupting ongoing reads or writes**. 🚀","locale":"en"},"925":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-14","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#why-optimization-is-needed","content":"`optimize()` **does not lock the collection**. Other threads and operations can continue reading, writing, and querying without delay while optimization is running — your application stays fully responsive.","locale":"en"},"926":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-15","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#check-indexing-status","content":"Use the `stats` property to get real-time insights into your collection's indexing state:","locale":"en"},"927":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-16","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#check-indexing-status","content":"`doc_count`: Total number of documents currently stored.","locale":"en"},"928":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-17","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#check-indexing-status","content":"`index_completeness`: Fraction (0.0\\~1.0) indicating how much of the vector data has been indexed.","locale":"en"},"929":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-18","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#check-indexing-status","content":"`1.0` → All vectors for that vector field are fully indexed using the configured index","locale":"en"},"930":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-19","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#check-indexing-status","content":"`0.0` → No indexing has occurred; all vectors remain in the flat buffer and are searched via brute force","locale":"en"},"931":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-20","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#check-indexing-status","content":"**Values in between** → Indexing is partial or in progress","locale":"en"},"932":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-21","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#when-to-call-optimize","content":"Optimize **regularly — but not too often**:","locale":"en"},"933":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-22","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#when-to-call-optimize","content":"**Too infrequent** → Flat buffers grow large, degrading search performance","locale":"en"},"934":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-23","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#when-to-call-optimize","content":"**Too frequent** → Wastes resources optimizing small batches prematurely","locale":"en"},"935":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-24","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#when-to-call-optimize","content":"Find a balance based on your **data ingestion rate** and **query latency requirements**.","locale":"en"},"936":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize-25","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/optimize#when-to-call-optimize","content":"**Best practice:**\\\nCheck your collection's indexing status if searches feel slow.\\\nAs a general guideline, consider optimizing when you have **100,000+ unindexed documents** — but adjust based on your specific use case.","locale":"en"},"937":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","type":"page","content":"Schema Evolution","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Products","Zvec","Guides","Collections"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","locale":"en"},"938":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-0","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#data-definition-language-ddl","content":"Data Definition Language (DDL)","locale":"en"},"939":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-1","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#prerequisites","content":"Prerequisites","locale":"en"},"940":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-2","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#column-ddl","content":"Column DDL","locale":"en"},"941":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-3","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#add-a-column","content":"Add a Column","locale":"en"},"942":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-4","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#drop-a-column","content":"Drop a Column","locale":"en"},"943":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-5","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#alter-a-column","content":"Alter a Column","locale":"en"},"944":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-6","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#view-the-current-schema","content":"View the Current Schema","locale":"en"},"945":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-7","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#index-ddl","content":"Index DDL","locale":"en"},"946":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-8","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#create-an-index","content":"Create an Index","locale":"en"},"947":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-9","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#drop-an-index","content":"Drop an Index","locale":"en"},"948":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-10","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","content":"Zvec supports **dynamic schema evolution**, allowing you to modify a collection's structure after it has been created — without downtime, data re-ingestion, or reindexing.","locale":"en"},"949":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-11","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","content":"You can:","locale":"en"},"950":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-12","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","content":"✅ **Add or drop scalar fields**","locale":"en"},"951":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-13","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","content":"✅ **Rename fields** or **change their data types** ((as long as the change is safe — e.g., from `INT32` to `INT64`)","locale":"en"},"952":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-14","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","content":"✅ **Create or drop indexes on fields**","locale":"en"},"953":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-15","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","content":"❌  Add or drop vector fields (🔜 coming soon)","locale":"en"},"954":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-16","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#data-definition-language-ddl","content":"In Zvec, schema changes are performed using &#x2A;*Data Definition Language (DDL)** methods, grouped into two categories:","locale":"en"},"955":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-17","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#data-definition-language-ddl","content":"**Column DDL**: defines *what data you store*.\\\nIt manages the structure of your collection by adding, removing, renaming or altering fields.","locale":"en"},"956":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-18","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#data-definition-language-ddl","content":"**Index DDL**: defines *how you search that data*.\\\nIt controls the creation and removal of indexes on fields.","locale":"en"},"957":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-19","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#data-definition-language-ddl","content":"💡 **Indexing Rules in Zvec**","locale":"en"},"958":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-20","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#data-definition-language-ddl","content":"**Every vector field must be indexed** using an appropriate vector index to enable similarity search.","locale":"en"},"959":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-21","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#data-definition-language-ddl","content":"**Scalar fields are optionally indexed** — but you should build inverted indexes on any scalar field you plan to use in filtering queries (e.g., `WHERE category = 'music'`).","locale":"en"},"960":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-22","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#prerequisites","content":"This guide assumes you have opened a collection and have a `collection` object ready.","locale":"en"},"961":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-23","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#prerequisites","content":"This example collection includes a scalar field `publish_year`.","locale":"en"},"962":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-24","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#add-a-column","content":"To add a new scalar field to an existing collection, use `add_column()`:","locale":"en"},"963":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-25","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#add-a-column","content":"`field_schema`:\\\nDefines the name and data type of the new field. See scalar field schema for details.","locale":"en"},"964":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-26","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#add-a-column","content":"`expression`:\\\nSpecifies the default value for existing documents. Since they don't already have a `rating` field, Zvec uses this `expression` to fill in the missing values — in this case, setting `rating = 5` for all current documents.","locale":"en"},"965":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-27","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#add-a-column","content":"Currently, only **numerical scalar fields** can be added via `add_column()`. Support for `string` and `boolean` types is coming soon.\\\nAccordingly, the `expression` must evaluate to a number — it can be a single numerical literal (like `\"5\"`) or a simple arithmetic expression involving existing numerical fields (e.g., `\"publish_year + 1\"`).","locale":"en"},"966":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-28","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#drop-a-column","content":"To permanently remove a scalar field, use `drop_column()`:","locale":"en"},"967":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-29","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#drop-a-column","content":"This **deletes the field and all its data from every document** in the collection. The operation is **irreversible**.","locale":"en"},"968":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-30","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#alter-a-column","content":"To rename a column or update its schema, use `alter_column()`:","locale":"en"},"969":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-31","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#view-the-current-schema","content":"After making changes, you can always check your collection's current structure by printing its schema:","locale":"en"},"970":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-32","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#view-the-current-schema","content":"See schema example for more details.","locale":"en"},"971":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-33","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#create-an-index","content":"To accelerate search performance, you can create (or replace) indexes on both **vector** and **scalar** fields using `create_index()`:","locale":"en"},"972":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-34","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#create-an-index","content":"**Vector fields** must use one of the following index types:","locale":"en"},"973":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-35","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#create-an-index","content":"`HnswIndexParam`","locale":"en"},"974":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-36","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#create-an-index","content":"`HnswRabitqIndexParam`","locale":"en"},"975":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-37","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#create-an-index","content":"`IVFIndexParam`","locale":"en"},"976":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-38","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#create-an-index","content":"`FlatIndexParam`","locale":"en"},"977":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-39","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#create-an-index","content":"**Scalar fields** use `InvertIndexParam` to enable efficient filtering.","locale":"en"},"978":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-40","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#drop-an-index","content":"To remove an index from a scalar field, use `drop_index()`:","locale":"en"},"979":{"id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution-41","page_id":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/collections/schema-evolution#drop-an-index","content":"**It is not allowed to drop the index of a vector field**.\\\nIn Zvec, every vector field must always have exactly one index to support similarity search.","locale":"en"},"980":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","type":"page","content":"Data Modeling","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Products","Zvec","Introduction","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","locale":"en"},"981":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-0","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","content":"How Zvec organizes data with collections, documents, schemas, and indexes.","locale":"en"},"982":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-1","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#collections","content":"Collections","locale":"en"},"983":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-2","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#why-use-collections","content":"Why Use Collections?","locale":"en"},"984":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-3","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#persistence","content":"Persistence","locale":"en"},"985":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-4","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#documents","content":"Documents","locale":"en"},"986":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-5","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#structure-of-a-document","content":"Structure of a Document","locale":"en"},"987":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-6","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#example-document","content":"Example Document","locale":"en"},"988":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-7","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#data-types","content":"Data Types","locale":"en"},"989":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-8","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"Scalar Types","locale":"en"},"990":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-9","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#vector-types","content":"Vector Types","locale":"en"},"991":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-10","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#indexes","content":"Indexes","locale":"en"},"992":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-11","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","content":"In Zvec, data is organized into **collections** and **documents**.","locale":"en"},"993":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-12","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#collections","content":"A **collection** is a named container for documents — similar to a **table** in a relational database system such as MySQL, where each **document** represents a **row** in a table. A collection is where you store, organize, and query your data.","locale":"en"},"994":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-13","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#collections","content":"Every collection is governed by a **schema** that defines the scalar fields and vectors it contains, along with their types and indexing settings.","locale":"en"},"995":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-14","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#collections","content":"**All documents within a collection conform to the same schema**.","locale":"en"},"996":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-15","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#collections","content":"The collection schema in Zvec is **dynamic**: you can add or remove scalar fields and vectors at any time without recreating the collection.","locale":"en"},"997":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-16","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#collections","content":"**No cross-collection queries**: Joins, unions, or multi-collection searches are **not supported**. Design your data model accordingly.","locale":"en"},"998":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-17","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#why-use-collections","content":"Collections provide **isolation** by ensuring that each use case operates with its own dedicated schema and index configuration. This separation prevents interference between unrelated use cases and allows each to evolve independently.","locale":"en"},"999":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-18","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#why-use-collections","content":"For example:","locale":"en"},"1000":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-19","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#why-use-collections","content":"A **Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) collection** might store text embeddings together with metadata — such as title, section, source URL, and last-updated timestamp.","locale":"en"},"1001":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-20","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#why-use-collections","content":"An **image search collection** could hold high-dimensional image embeddings along with associated fields like image ID, file path, or caption.","locale":"en"},"1002":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-21","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#persistence","content":"**Each collection is persisted independently on disk in its own dedicated directory**, providing isolation between different use cases.","locale":"en"},"1003":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-22","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#persistence","content":"Each collection is **self-contained within its directory**. This means you can relocate a collection's directory and Zvec will still be able to open it when provided with the correct path.","locale":"en"},"1004":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-23","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#documents","content":"A document is the fundamental unit of data storage — think of it as a single record or row in a relational database table. Each document lives inside a collection and must conform to that collection's schema.","locale":"en"},"1005":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-24","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#structure-of-a-document","content":"A document is a **structured** object composed of three core components.","locale":"en"},"1006":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-25","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#structure-of-a-document","content":"🔑 `id`: A unique string identifier for the document, cannot be changed after insertion","locale":"en"},"1007":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-26","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#structure-of-a-document","content":"📐 `vectors`: A named set of vectors","locale":"en"},"1008":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-27","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#structure-of-a-document","content":"🗂️ `fields`: A named set of scalar (non-vector) fields, which can include strings, numbers, booleans, or arrays of these types","locale":"en"},"1009":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-28","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#example-document","content":"This document belongs to a collection with a schema that defines:","locale":"en"},"1010":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-29","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#example-document","content":"Two dense vector: `vector_1` (4-dimensional) and `vector_2` (6-dimensional)","locale":"en"},"1011":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-30","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#example-document","content":"One sparse vector: `vector_3`","locale":"en"},"1012":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-31","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#example-document","content":"Scalar fields: `category` (string), `price` (integer), and `languages` (array of strings)","locale":"en"},"1013":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-32","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#example-document","content":"**All fields must conform to their declared types in the schema**. Vectors must exactly match the specified type (dense or sparse) and dimensionality (e.g., a 768-dimensional dense vector cannot accept a 512-dimensional vector).","locale":"en"},"1014":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-33","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#example-document","content":"Once inserted, documents can be updated via `upsert()` or partial `update()` operations, but all modifications must still adhere to the collection's schema constraints.","locale":"en"},"1015":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-34","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#data-types","content":"Zvec uses a strongly typed schema system based on the `DataType` enumeration. The supported types fall into two categories:","locale":"en"},"1016":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-35","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#data-types","content":"**Scalar types** — strings, integers, floats, booleans, and arrays of these types","locale":"en"},"1017":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-36","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#data-types","content":"**Vector types** — dense or sparse numeric representations for vector embeddings","locale":"en"},"1018":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-37","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#data-types","content":"**Type safety is enforced at ingestion**: every field within a document must strictly conform to its declared `DataType`.","locale":"en"},"1019":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-38","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"Elementary Types","locale":"en"},"1020":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-39","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"`STRING`","locale":"en"},"1021":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-40","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"`BOOL`","locale":"en"},"1022":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-41","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"`INT32`","locale":"en"},"1023":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-42","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"`INT64`","locale":"en"},"1024":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-43","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"`UINT32`","locale":"en"},"1025":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-44","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"`UINT64`","locale":"en"},"1026":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-45","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"`FLOAT`","locale":"en"},"1027":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-46","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"`DOUBLE`","locale":"en"},"1028":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-47","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"Array Types","locale":"en"},"1029":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-48","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"`ARRAY_STRING`","locale":"en"},"1030":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-49","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"`ARRAY_BOOL`","locale":"en"},"1031":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-50","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"`ARRAY_INT32`","locale":"en"},"1032":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-51","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"`ARRAY_INT64`","locale":"en"},"1033":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-52","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"`ARRAY_UINT32`","locale":"en"},"1034":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-53","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"`ARRAY_UINT64`","locale":"en"},"1035":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-54","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"`ARRAY_FLOAT`","locale":"en"},"1036":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-55","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"`ARRAY_DOUBLE`","locale":"en"},"1037":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-56","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#scalar-types","content":"Arrays cannot contain mixed types or nested structures. All elements must match the declared array element type.","locale":"en"},"1038":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-57","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#vector-types","content":"Dense Vector: represented as fixed-length numeric arrays, e.g., `[0.1, -0.5, ..., 0.9]`","locale":"en"},"1039":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-58","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#vector-types","content":"`VECTOR_FP16`","locale":"en"},"1040":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-59","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#vector-types","content":"`VECTOR_FP32`","locale":"en"},"1041":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-60","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#vector-types","content":"`VECTOR_INT8`","locale":"en"},"1042":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-61","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#vector-types","content":"Sparse Vector: represented as maps from integer indices to float values, e.g., `{ 42: 0.85, 1024: 0.13 }`","locale":"en"},"1043":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-62","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#vector-types","content":"`SPARSE_VECTOR_FP32`","locale":"en"},"1044":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-63","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#vector-types","content":"`SPARSE_VECTOR_FP16`","locale":"en"},"1045":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-64","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#indexes","content":"Indexes accelerate data retrieval beyond basic storage of scalar fields and vectors. In Zvec:","locale":"en"},"1046":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-65","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#indexes","content":"**Every vector field must be indexed** using an appropriate vector index to enable similarity search.","locale":"en"},"1047":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-66","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#indexes","content":"**Scalar fields are optionally indexed** — but you should build inverted indexes on any scalar field you plan to use in filtering queries (e.g., `WHERE category = 'music'`).","locale":"en"},"1048":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling-67","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/data-modeling#indexes","content":"You can define indexes at collection creation by specifying `index_param` in the schema for each field or vector.\\\nAlternatively, you can add indexes after collection creation by calling `create_index()` dynamically — no data re-ingestion required.","locale":"en"},"1049":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","type":"page","content":"Full-Text Index","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Products","Zvec","Introduction","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","locale":"en"},"1050":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-0","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","content":"An inverted-index-based text retrieval mechanism — supports natural language queries, phrase matching, and boolean expressions, ranked by BM25 relevance scoring.","locale":"en"},"1051":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-1","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#when-to-use-a-full-text-index","content":"When to Use a Full-Text Index","locale":"en"},"1052":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-2","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#how-does-it-work","content":"How Does It Work?","locale":"en"},"1053":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-3","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#1-tokenization","content":"Tokenization","locale":"en"},"1054":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-4","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#2-token-filtering","content":"Token Filtering","locale":"en"},"1055":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-5","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#3-building-the-inverted-map","content":"Building the Inverted Map","locale":"en"},"1056":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-6","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#4-bm25-scoring","content":"BM25 Scoring","locale":"en"},"1057":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-7","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#5-wand-optimization","content":"WAND Optimization","locale":"en"},"1058":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-8","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#tokenizers","content":"Tokenizers","locale":"en"},"1059":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-9","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#key-parameters","content":"Key Parameters","locale":"en"},"1060":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-10","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#index-time-parameters","content":"Index-Time Parameters","locale":"en"},"1061":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-11","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#query-time-parameters","content":"Query-Time Parameters","locale":"en"},"1062":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-12","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#full-text-index-vs-vector-index","content":"Full-Text Index vs. Vector Index","locale":"en"},"1063":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-13","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#trade-offs","content":"Trade-offs","locale":"en"},"1064":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-14","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","content":"A full-text index is a data structure designed for &#x2A;**efficient keyword-based search over text content***.","locale":"en"},"1065":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-15","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","content":"It breaks text fields into terms (tokens), builds an inverted mapping from terms to documents, and uses BM25 scoring to return results **ranked by relevance** — without scanning every document.","locale":"en"},"1066":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-16","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#when-to-use-a-full-text-index","content":"Use a full-text index when you need to **search text content by keywords and rank results by relevance**. It excels in scenarios such as:","locale":"en"},"1067":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-17","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#when-to-use-a-full-text-index","content":"✅ Natural language queries: users type everyday language to find content","locale":"en"},"1068":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-18","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#when-to-use-a-full-text-index","content":"✅ Exact phrase matching: `\"vector database\"` matches the complete phrase, not individual words","locale":"en"},"1069":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-19","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#when-to-use-a-full-text-index","content":"✅ Boolean retrieval: `+machine -neural` to require or exclude specific terms","locale":"en"},"1070":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-20","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#when-to-use-a-full-text-index","content":"✅ Multi-language support: built-in tokenizers for English-like languages and Chinese","locale":"en"},"1071":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-21","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index#when-to-use-a-full-text-index","content":"✅ Text-only use cases: build a full-text search collection with no vector fields at all","locale":"en"},"1072":{"id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts-index-22","page_id":"/en/docs/db/concepts/fts